This assumes the recording is NDJSON, one request per line. I don't know your actual format, so adapt `parseLine` if it differs (HAR, access logs, etc.):

```json
{"ts":1700000000123,"method":"POST","path":"/v1/orders?x=1","headers":{"content-type":"application/json"},"body":"{\"a\":1}"}
```

`ts` is epoch milliseconds. Use `bodyBase64` instead of `body` for binary payloads.

## replay.mjs

```js
// npm i undici   (Node 18+)
import { createReadStream } from 'node:fs';
import { createInterface } from 'node:readline';
import { Pool } from 'undici';

const {
  TARGET = 'http://staging.internal:8080',
  FILE = 'traffic.ndjson',
  SPEED = '1',            // 1 = real time, 4 = 4x faster
  LOOPS = '3',            // 0 = loop forever
  MAX_INFLIGHT = '500',   // shed load instead of piling up if staging stalls
  AUTH_HEADER = '',       // optional: override Authorization, e.g. "Bearer abc"
} = process.env;

const speed = Number(SPEED);
const maxLoops = Number(LOOPS);
const maxInflight = Number(MAX_INFLIGHT);

const pool = new Pool(TARGET, { connections: 100, pipelining: 1 });

const DROP_HEADERS = new Set([
  'host', 'content-length', 'connection', 'transfer-encoding',
  'keep-alive', 'upgrade', 'accept-encoding',
]);

const stats = { sent: 0, dropped: 0, errors: 0, inflight: 0, status: {} };

function parseLine(line) {
  const r = JSON.parse(line);
  const headers = {};
  for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(r.headers ?? {})) {
    if (!DROP_HEADERS.has(k.toLowerCase())) headers[k] = v;
  }
  if (AUTH_HEADER) headers.authorization = AUTH_HEADER;
  const body = r.bodyBase64 ? Buffer.from(r.bodyBase64, 'base64') : r.body;
  return { ts: r.ts, method: r.method, path: r.path, headers, body };
}

async function fire(req) {
  stats.inflight++;
  stats.sent++;
  try {
    const res = await pool.request({
      method: req.method,
      path: req.path,
      headers: req.headers,
      body: req.body,
      headersTimeout: 30_000,
      bodyTimeout: 30_000,
    });
    await res.body.dump(); // always drain, or connections stall
    stats.status[res.statusCode] = (stats.status[res.statusCode] ?? 0) + 1;
  } catch (e) {
    stats.errors++;
  } finally {
    stats.inflight--;
  }
}

const sleep = (ms) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));

async function playOnce(loop) {
  const rl = createInterface({ input: createReadStream(FILE), crlfDelay: Infinity });
  let firstTs = null;
  const start = Date.now();

  for await (const line of rl) {
    if (!line.trim()) continue;
    let req;
    try { req = parseLine(line); } catch { continue; }

    firstTs ??= req.ts;
    const due = start + (req.ts - firstTs) / speed;
    const wait = due - Date.now();
    if (wait > 0) await sleep(wait);

    if (stats.inflight >= maxInflight) { stats.dropped++; continue; }
    fire(req); // intentionally not awaited: open-loop, preserves arrival rate
  }
  console.log(`loop ${loop} finished in ${((Date.now() - start) / 1000).toFixed(0)}s`);
}

const reporter = setInterval(() => {
  console.log(new Date().toISOString(), JSON.stringify(stats));
}, 10_000);

for (let i = 1; maxLoops === 0 || i <= maxLoops; i++) {
  await playOnce(i);
}

while (stats.inflight > 0) await sleep(200);
clearInterval(reporter);
console.log('done', JSON.stringify(stats));
await pool.close();
```

Run it somewhere that survives your shell closing:

```bash
npm i undici
TARGET=http://staging.internal:8080 FILE=traffic.ndjson LOOPS=3 \
  nohup node replay.mjs > replay.log 2>&1 &
```

## Design choices

- **Open-loop timing.** Requests fire at their recorded offsets whether or not earlier ones have finished, which matches real traffic. A closed-loop replay (wait for each response) would hide problems by slowing itself down. `MAX_INFLIGHT` sheds load only if staging falls far behind, and `dropped` in the stats tells you if that happened. If it's nonzero, your replay isn't representative.
- **Looping.** One recorded hour covers only one snapshot interval. `LOOPS=3` gives roughly 3 hours, enough for a warm-up snapshot plus two an hour apart. Start the first snapshot after the first loop's warm-up, not at second zero.
- **`SPEED`.** If the leak is per-request, `SPEED=3` reproduces growth faster. Verify this against production growth per request rather than assuming it, because time-based leaks (timers, TTL caches, connection-age effects) won't speed up the same way. Start at 1x if in doubt.

## Check before you run it

- **Side effects.** Replayed POST/PUT/DELETE requests will hit whatever staging is connected to: databases, payment sandboxes, email/SMS providers, webhooks. Confirm those are stubbed or disposable.
- **Stale credentials.** Recorded tokens and sessions have probably expired. If most responses are 401/403, you're only exercising the auth path. Use `AUTH_HEADER` with a long-lived staging token, or rewrite auth in `parseLine`. Watch the `status` counts in the first minute.
- **Duplicate-key conflicts on loops 2+.** Replayed creates with client-supplied IDs or idempotency keys will return 409s on later loops. Rewrite those fields per loop if that matters, since you want the same code paths as production.
- **Sensitive data.** Recordings contain real headers and bodies. Make sure the file is scrubbed or handled as production data.
- **Realistic status mix.** Compare the replay's status distribution to production. A leak on a success path won't show up if everything errors early, and vice versa.

Run the `process.memoryUsage()` logging from earlier alongside this. After the first 10-15 minutes of replay you should already see whether `heapUsed` or `rss` is climbing, which tells you whether the replay reproduces the problem before you spend hours waiting on snapshots.

