This question assumes the device has a transmit trigger (every N samples, when the buffer fills, or something else), and you haven't chosen one. It matters because the trigger sets how often the radio powers on, which is what decides whether you reach six months. The sketch below uses a default you can change: transmit every 10 samples, or early if the buffer is 75% full, with backoff after failures. It also assumes a simple prototype, so it survives power loss by restarting with an empty buffer rather than saving to flash.

```c
#include <time.h>
#include "esp_sleep.h"
#include "esp_attr.h"

#define SAMPLE_PERIOD_S   60
#define TX_EVERY_N        10                      /* normal trigger */
#define TX_HIGH_WATER     (BUF_CAPACITY * 3 / 4)  /* early-send trigger */
#define BACKOFF_MAX_S     (60 * 60)

static RTC_DATA_ATTR uint32_t s_next_tx_ok_s;     /* don't try before this time */
static RTC_DATA_ATTR uint32_t s_backoff_s;        /* current retry delay */

static bool should_transmit(uint32_t now)
{
    uint16_t n = readings_count();
    if (now < s_next_tx_ok_s) return false;       /* still backing off */
    return n >= TX_EVERY_N || n >= TX_HIGH_WATER;
}

static void do_transmit(uint32_t now)
{
    uint16_t sent = 0;
    reading_t r;

    if (radio_connect() == ESP_OK) {
        while (readings_get(sent, &r) && radio_send(&r) == ESP_OK) sent++;
        radio_disconnect();
    }
    readings_discard(sent);

    if (readings_count() == 0) {
        s_backoff_s = 0;                          /* success: reset backoff */
        s_next_tx_ok_s = 0;
    } else {
        /* failure: 2 min, 4, 8 ... capped at 1 h, so a dead link
           doesn't burn the battery on retries every minute */
        s_backoff_s = s_backoff_s ? s_backoff_s * 2 : 120;
        if (s_backoff_s > BACKOFF_MAX_S) s_backoff_s = BACKOFF_MAX_S;
        s_next_tx_ok_s = now + s_backoff_s;
    }
}

void app_main(void)
{
    readings_init();                              /* no-op after deep-sleep wake */

    uint32_t now = (uint32_t)time(NULL);
    readings_add(now, read_sensor_scaled());      /* radio stays off here */

    if (should_transmit(now)) {
        do_transmit(now);
    }

    /* Optional: subtract time spent awake so the period doesn't creep.
       Skipped here; with a 60 s period the error is small. */
    ESP_ERROR_CHECK(set_wake_timer(SAMPLE_PERIOD_S));
    esp_deep_sleep_start();                       /* never returns */
}
```

Each cycle goes: wake (reset), sample, transmit only if the trigger says so, arm the timer, and deep sleep. The radio is powered only inside `do_transmit()`.

**Rough power check** (my assumptions, so measure yours):
- Transmit: about 4 s at about 100 mA every 10 minutes averages roughly 0.7 mA.
- Sample wakes: about 0.1 s at about 40 mA every minute averages roughly 0.07 mA.
- Sleep plus regulator and sensor quiescent current: perhaps 0.01 to 0.1 mA.

That totals around 0.8 to 0.9 mA, or about 9 to 10 months on the roughly 5.8 Ah battery you implied. This is inside your 1.3 mA budget but not by a huge margin. Slow reconnects, retries, or a leaky regulator will eat it. If you can cut the connect time, or transmit every 20 to 30 minutes, you gain headroom.

If you'd rather use a different trigger, such as a fixed time interval or "only when a value changes," only `should_transmit()` needs to change. If you need readings to survive a brownout, tell me and I'll add a batched flash write.

