```c
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

#define SLEEP_INTERVAL_S    60
#define RADIO_WAKE_MS       5      // check datasheet: oscillator/PLL settle time
#define TX_TIMEOUT_MS       500
#define MAX_TX_RETRIES      2

typedef struct {
    int16_t  temperature_c_x100;
    uint16_t battery_mv;
    // ... your sensor fields
} reading_t;

int main(void)
{
    board_init();          // clocks, GPIO, unused pins to low-power state
    rtc_timer_init();      // low-power timer that can wake the MCU from deep sleep
    watchdog_init();       // guards against hangs that would burn the battery

    while (1) {
        // 1. Sample sensors (power them only for as long as needed)
        sensor_power(true);
        reading_t r = sensor_read();
        sensor_power(false);

        // 2. Wake the radio and reconfigure it
        radio_wake();
        delay_ms(RADIO_WAKE_MS);
        radio_configure();         // many radios lose config in deep sleep

        // 3. Transmit with a bounded retry
        bool ok = false;
        for (int i = 0; i <= MAX_TX_RETRIES && !ok; i++) {
            radio_send(&r, sizeof r);
            ok = radio_wait_tx_done(TX_TIMEOUT_MS);
            watchdog_kick();
        }

        // 4. Put the radio to sleep no matter what happened
        radio_sleep();

        // 5. Put the MCU to sleep until the timer fires
        rtc_timer_set(SLEEP_INTERVAL_S);
        watchdog_kick();
        mcu_deep_sleep();          // execution resumes here after the RTC interrupt
    }
}
```

**Things that commonly undermine the savings:**

- **The MCU also has to sleep.** If you only sleep the radio and leave the MCU spinning in a delay loop, you'll still draw several mA. Use a real deep-sleep mode with an RTC or low-power timer wake source.
- **Radio state after wake.** Check whether your radio retains its configuration in sleep. If not, reconfigure it every cycle, as above, and include that time in your energy budget.
- **Bound every wait.** A missed TX-done flag shouldn't leave the radio on indefinitely. Timeouts and a watchdog matter more here than in mains-powered designs.
- **Leakage in sleep.** Floating GPIOs, pull-ups on active-low lines, sensor power rails, and a voltage regulator with high quiescent current can each dominate once the radio is off. Measure sleep current directly.
- **Wake-on-timer drift.** Internal RC oscillators drift with temperature. If timing matters, use a 32 kHz crystal for the RTC.

**Rough energy budget:** If a cycle keeps the radio on for about 100 ms at 80 mA every 60 s, the radio averages roughly 0.13 mA. Add MCU and sleep current, and you might land around 0.15 to 0.2 mA total, versus 80 mA now. That would be hundreds of times longer battery life, though real results depend on wake time, retries, and sleep leakage.

